body: "## Measurement\n\n`pm events` emits newline-delimited JSON, one object per committed mutation fact. Each row carries a durable resume cursor as its first field:\n\n```json\n{\"cursor\":\"eyJ2ZXJzaW9uIjoxLCJmaW5nZXJwcmludCI6IjQ0MTM2ZmEzNTViMzY3OGExMTQ2YWQxNiIsInRzIjoiMjAyNi0wNy0yNlQwNToyOTozMS4wMjFaIiwic3RyZWFtX2lkIjoic2J4LXRubHgiLCJzdHJlYW1fb2Zmc2V0IjowfQ\",\"item_id\":\"sbx-tnlx\",\"version\":1,\"ts\":\"2026-07-26T05:29:31.021Z\",\"author\":\"harness:claude-code\",\"type\":\"create\",\"message\":\"\",\"patch_count\":10}\n```\n\nMeasured 2026-07-26 on the installed 2026.7.26 build:\n\n| corpus | events | total bytes | cursor bytes | cursor share |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| this repository's workspace | 200 | 69,766 | 33,667 | **48%** |\n| seeded scratch workspace | 10 | 3,204 | 1,710 | **53%** |\n\nAt roughly four bytes per token, a 200-event read spends about **8,400 tokens on resume cursors** to deliver about 9,000 tokens of facts. The cursor is a base64 envelope of a small object — `{version, fingerprint, ts, stream_id, stream_offset}` — every field of which is either constant across the batch (`version`, `fingerprint`) or already present in the row as plaintext (`ts`, `stream_id` = `item_id`). The only field not otherwise recoverable is `stream_offset`.\n\n## The structural point\n\nAn agent resuming a stream needs **one** cursor: the last one it durably processed. It never needs the other 199. The current encoding charges per row for a capability consumed once per batch, and it charges in the most expensive possible form — an opaque base64 blob, which a tokenizer cannot compress and an agent cannot read, reason about, or truncate safely.\n\nThe cost is not incidental to the scale story, it is proportional to it. `pm events` is the fleet-coordination primitive: it is the surface a thousand agents poll to learn what changed, and the surface a derived read index would tail to stay current. Its per-row overhead is multiplied by exactly the factor the scale milestone is trying to make affordable.\n\n## Options, with the trade-off stated honestly\n\n1. **Batch-terminal cursor (recommended default).** Emit rows without cursors and close the batch with a single terminal record carrying the resume cursor. Preserves exact resumability, removes essentially all of the overhead, and is the conventional shape for cursor-paginated NDJSON. Cost: a consumer that dies mid-batch resumes from the previous batch boundary and re-reads at most `--limit` events. Since every consumer of an event stream must already be idempotent — the stream can replay after any crash — this is not a new requirement.\n2. **Opt-in per-row cursors.** Keep the current behaviour behind an explicit flag for consumers that genuinely commit per row and cannot tolerate replay. Both shapes remain available; the expensive one stops being the default.\n3. **Structured cursor instead of base64.** If per-row cursors are retained, emit the cursor as a compact structured field with the redundant components elided, so the wire form carries only `stream_offset` plus the batch-constant fingerprint emitted once in a header. Cheaper than base64 and legible, but still pays per row.\n\nThe recommendation is (1) as the default with (2) available, because it is the only option that removes the cost rather than reducing it.\n\n## Compatibility\n\n`pm events` cursors are durable and documented as resumable across versions. Any change must keep an already-issued cursor accepted by `--since` indefinitely — the encoding of *emitted* cursors is what changes, not the parser. The parser therefore stays permissive and the change is one-directional, which is exactly the compatibility shape `pm-pae3wo` requires for cross-version workspace reads.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n- A declared, measured, and gated per-fact token cost for `pm events` in both shapes, so the number in the table above becomes a regression-tested budget rather than a one-time observation.\n- The default emission shape carries resume capability once per batch.\n- Already-issued cursors remain accepted by `--since` with a test that pins an old-form cursor literal.\n- `--follow` gets the same treatment: the follow loop emits a cursor at each batch boundary rather than per row, which also gives a natural heartbeat for an idle stream.\n\n## Relationship to neighbours\n\n- `pm-e200` (closed) built the event stream and its durable cursor. This is a token-efficiency defect in the shipped encoding, recorded as `discovered_from` so the escape is attributable rather than re-litigated as a feature request.\n- `pm-kvxqp1` is the general study — measure every output encoding against an information-equivalence baseline and adopt the cheapest per surface. This is the single sharpest instance found so far and should be one of its worked examples, but it does not need to wait for the study: the redundancy here is provable by inspection, not by measurement across candidates.\n- `pm-5t33or` declares per-command output budgets. `pm events` is the surface where a budget is most obviously needed and least obviously bounded, since the stream is unbounded by design.\n- `pm-t5dt4z` makes any command able to report its own token cost. That instrument is what turns this item's table into a standing gate.\n- `pm-mav1ak` requires every row-producing read to stream incrementally in constant memory. A batch-terminal cursor interacts with that contract and the two must agree on where a batch boundary is; recorded as `related` so the boundary definition is settled once.\n- `pm-801d` (derived read index) is a first-class consumer of this stream and the one whose cost scales with workspace size rather than with agent count."
